All paths are absolute. The vendored (build-integrated) copies under `CHAOS/gem5/src/...` are the ones actually compiled; the parallel top-level `CHAOS/CHAOSReg/` and `CHAOS/CHAOSPhysReg/` are the same sources mirrored.

## 1. The ORIGINAL injector — `CHAOSReg`

**Sources (vendored, canonical):**
- `/home/sdc/gem5-fi/CHAOS/gem5/src/CHAOSReg/CHAOSReg.hh`
- `/home/sdc/gem5-fi/CHAOS/gem5/src/CHAOSReg/CHAOSReg.cc`
- `/home/sdc/gem5-fi/CHAOS/gem5/src/CHAOSReg/CHAOSReg.py`

### Injection lifecycle point — timer-driven, not a CPU pipeline hook

`CHAOSReg` does **not** hook any O3 pipeline stage. It is a `SimObject` that schedules a gem5 `Event` (`attackEvent`) on the CPU's event queue. The event fires `attackCheck()` (CHAOSReg.cc:275), which loops over threads and calls `processFault(tid)` (CHAOSReg.cc:163). So injection happens in the global event loop, not at commit/rename/decode.

```cpp
// CHAOSReg.cc:275-289
void 
CHAOSReg::attackCheck()
{
    if (!probability)
        return;

    for (ThreadID tid = 0; tid < cpu->numThreads; ++tid) {
        ThreadContext *thread_context = cpu->getContext(tid);
        if (!thread_context || thread_context->status() == ThreadContext::Halted) {
            continue;
        }

        if (PC_target == 0 || (PC_target == thread_context->pcState().instAddr())) {
            processFault(tid);
        }
    }
    ...
}
```

Rescheduling is geometric: `Cycles next_injection = Cycles(inter_fault_cycles_dist(rng));` then `scheduleAttackEvent(next_injection);` (CHAOSReg.cc:310, 323-325). So "when" = `firstClock` + a geometric(p) offset, repeating.

### Mapping arch→phys — it does NOT explicitly map; it goes through `ThreadContext`

This is the key correctness issue. `CHAOSReg` samples an **architectural** register index uniformly from `regClasses()[IntRegClass]->numRegs()`, builds a `RegId`, and calls `ThreadContext::getReg/setReg`:

```cpp
// CHAOSReg.cc:210-219
    int num_regs = reg_class->numRegs();
    int upper = num_regs - 1;
    if (max_reg_idx > 0 && max_reg_idx < num_regs)
        upper = max_reg_idx - 1;

    int random_reg = std::uniform_int_distribution<>(0, upper)(rng);
    gem5::RegId reg_id(*reg_class, random_reg);

    try {
        gem5::RegVal reg_val = thread_context->getReg(reg_id);
```

```cpp
// CHAOSReg.cc:250
        thread_context->setReg(reg_id, reg_val);
```

For an in-order CPU this writes the live architectural register. For an **O3** CPU, `ThreadContext::setReg` routes through the **commit-stage rename map** (`commitRenameMap`) — i.e. it writes the *committed* physical register for that arch reg. The in-flight (front-end `renameMap`) mapping and any already-renamed-but-uncommitted instructions keep their old source physical register. The code comment in `CHAOSPhysReg.cc` states this explicitly:

```cpp
// CHAOSPhysReg.cc:143-145
    // This is the key difference from CHAOSReg (which wrote the commitRenameMap
    // phys reg via ThreadContext::setReg — a backdoor that doesn't propagate
    // to in-flight instructions on O3).
```

So CHAOSReg on O3 = "commit-map arch" abstraction (memory note #1, confirmed). It does not perform an explicit rename-map lookup; the resolution happens implicitly inside `ThreadContext::setReg`.

### Stuck-at-0 / stuck-at-1 — write-path mask applied ONCE at inject, then re-applied periodically by a second event

At injection (CHAOSReg.cc:229-241) the mask is applied once and a `PermanentFault` entry is stored with `update = true`:

```cpp
// CHAOSReg.cc:229-241
            switch (chosen_fault_type_enum) {
                case FaultType::StuckAtZero:
                    reg_val &= ~mask;
                    stats->numStuckAtZero++;
                    stats->numPermanentFaults++;
                    permanent_faults[std::make_pair(tid, reg_id)] = {chosen_fault_type_enum, mask, true};
                    break;
                case FaultType::StuckAtOne:
                    reg_val |= mask;
                    stats->numStuckAtOne++;
                    stats->numPermanentFaults++;
                    permanent_faults[std::make_pair(tid, reg_id)] = {chosen_fault_type_enum, mask, true};
                    break;
                case FaultType::BitFlip:
                    reg_val ^= mask;
                    stats->numBitFlips++;
                    break;
                default:
                    break;
            }
```

A **second event** `periodicCheck` (scheduled every `cyclesPermamentFaultCheck` cycles, default 1) re-reads, re-applies the mask, and re-writes:

```cpp
// CHAOSReg.cc:332-380 (key part)
void 
CHAOSReg::checkPermanent()
{
    for (auto &entry : permanent_faults) {
        if (!entry.second.update)
            continue;
        ...
        gem5::RegVal reg_val = thread_context->getReg(reg_id);

        switch (fault.fault_type) {
            case FaultType::StuckAtZero:
                reg_val &= ~fault.mask;
                break;
            case FaultType::StuckAtOne:
                reg_val |= fault.mask;
                break;
            default:
                break;
        }

        thread_context->setReg(reg_id, reg_val);
        // NOTE: the original code cleared `update = false` here, which
        // meant the stuck mask was re-applied only ONCE after injection.
        // Any subsequent program write to this register would overwrite
        // the stuck value and the fault would silently disappear — so
        // "stuck_at_zero/one" were NOT actually permanent, under-applying
        // the fault model. Leaving `update = true` so checkPermanent
        // re-applies the mask every cyclesPermamedFaultCheck cycles,
        // making the stuck fault truly permanent ...
```

So: in the **current code** (this repo), `update` is left `true` and the mask is re-applied every `cyclesPermamedFaultCheck` cycles (default 1) — a periodic re-apply. The comment explains that the upstream/original CHAOS set `update = false` after the first re-apply, so the fault was NOT actually permanent (a bug). This repo fixes that. It is still a periodic re-apply, NOT a write-path mask hook on `setReg` — so any write between two periodic checks still briefly overwrites the stuck value (visible until the next periodic check).

