## 2. The NEW injector — `CHAOSPhysReg`

**Sources (vendored, canonical, O3-only):**
- `/home/sdc/gem5-fi/CHAOS/gem5/src/cpu/o3/CHAOSPhysReg/CHAOSPhysReg.hh`
- `/home/sdc/gem5-fi/CHAOS/gem5/src/cpu/o3/CHAOSPhysReg/CHAOSPhysReg.cc`
- `/home/sdc/gem5-fi/CHAOS/gem5/src/cpu/o3/CHAOSPhysReg/CHAOSPhysReg.py`
- `/home/sdc/gem5-fi/CHAOS/gem5/src/cpu/o3/CHAOSPhysReg/SConscript` (build)

### Mode enum — three modes

```cpp
// CHAOSPhysReg.hh:31
    enum class Mode { Phys, ArchFrontend, ArchCommit };
```

String mapping (CHAOSPhysReg.cc:100-105):

```cpp
// CHAOSPhysReg.cc:100-105
    CHAOSPhysReg::Mode
    CHAOSPhysReg::stringToMode(const std::string &s) {
        if (s == "arch_commit") return Mode::ArchCommit;
        else if (s == "arch_frontend") return Mode::ArchFrontend;
        return Mode::Phys;
    }
```

- **`phys`** (default): inject by **physical register index directly** — `targetPhysRegIdx`, or random over `[0, numIntPhysRegs-1]`. This is the ITC'23/GeFIN abstraction.
- **`arch_frontend`**: resolve the arch reg through the **front-end rename map** (`renameMap.lookup`) — the mapping in-flight instructions will read.
- **`arch_commit`**: resolve the arch reg through the **commit rename map** (`commitRenameMap.lookup`) — reproduces original CHAOSReg behavior; explicitly noted as "FAILS on O3, kept for comparison" in CHAOSPhysReg.py:4-6.

### How it resolves the register to inject — the `processFault` core

```cpp
// CHAOSPhysReg.cc:147-226 (key resolution paths)
void
CHAOSPhysReg::processFault(ThreadID tid)
{
    // 1. Resolve target physical register id.
    PhysRegIdPtr phys_reg = nullptr;
    int chosen_phys_idx = -1;     // for logging
    int chosen_arch_idx = -1;     // for logging (arch modes)

    if (fi_mode == Mode::Phys) {
        // Inject by physical register index. ...
        int n = cpu->physRegFile().numIntPhysRegs();
        if (n <= 0) return;
        chosen_phys_idx = (target_phys_idx >= 0)
            ? target_phys_idx
            : std::uniform_int_distribution<>(0, n - 1)(rng);
        if (chosen_phys_idx >= n) return;
        phys_reg = cpu->physRegFile().intPhysRegId(chosen_phys_idx);
        ...
    } else {
        // arch_frontend: renameMap.lookup      (in-flight mapping)
        // arch_commit:   commitRenameMap.lookup (committed mapping = CHAOSReg)
        ...
        int arch_idx = target_arch_idx;
        ...
        gem5::RegId arch_reg(*reg_class, arch_idx);
        // flatten to match the per-class rename map's indexing
        const gem5::RegId flat = arch_reg.flatten(*isa);
        if (fi_mode == Mode::ArchFrontend)
            phys_reg = cpu->frontRenameMap()[tid].lookup(flat);
        else // ArchCommit
            phys_reg = cpu->commitRenameMapAccess()[tid].lookup(flat);
        if (!phys_reg) return;
        chosen_phys_idx = phys_reg->index();
    }
    ...
}
```

After resolving `phys_reg`, it does the actual read-modify-write **directly on the physical register file** (CHAOSPhysReg.cc:231-263), bypassing `ThreadContext`:

```cpp
// CHAOSPhysReg.cc:231, 263
    gem5::RegVal reg_val = cpu->physRegFile().getReg(phys_reg);
    ...
    cpu->physRegFile().setReg(phys_reg, reg_val);
```

This is the fundamental difference from CHAOSReg: the write lands on the physical cell, so in-flight instructions that will read this phys reg later (via the front-end rename map) will see the corrupted value. In `arch_commit` mode the lookup still uses the commit map, but the write goes to the phys file directly — which is why even `arch_commit` differs subtly from CHAOSReg's `ThreadContext::setReg`.

### Liveness probe (phys mode only)

For `phys` mode, CHAOSPhysReg also probes whether the chosen phys slot is currently free (dead/inactive) using `UnifiedFreeList::isFree` — this is a key difference from random arch-reg sampling, because a phys slot may be unallocated at inject time:

```cpp
// CHAOSPhysReg.cc:174-203 (key part)
            bool is_free = cpu->physFreeList().isFree(
                gem5::IntRegClass, phys_reg);
            free_list_size_at_inject = cpu->physFreeList().numFreeRegs(
                gem5::IntRegClass);
            if (!is_free) {
                // active — also record which arch reg (if any) maps to it now,
                // for diagnostics (walk rename map; best-effort, not the
                // liveness source of truth).
                ...
                for (int a = 0; a < cap; a++) {   // cap = X0-X30
                    gem5::RegId ar(*ic, a);
                    const gem5::RegId flat = ar.flatten(*isa);
                    PhysRegIdPtr pm = cpu->frontRenameMap()[tid].lookup(flat);
                    if (pm && pm->index() == chosen_phys_idx) {
                        chosen_arch_idx = a;
                        break;
                    }
                }
            } else {
                chosen_arch_idx = -2;  // sentinel: free/inactive
            }
```

The reverse walk (front-end rename map scan) is **diagnostic only** — the source of truth for liveness is the free-list probe.

### Stuck fault mechanism in CHAOSPhysReg

Same `permanent_faults` map + `checkPermanent` periodic re-apply (CHAOSPhysReg.cc:339-367), keyed by **phys idx** (not arch RegId). The code itself states this is the **wrong** mechanism and that the correct one (write-path mask hook in `PhysRegFile::setReg`) is a **TODO**:

```cpp
// CHAOSPhysReg.cc:339-348
void
CHAOSPhysReg::checkPermanent()
{
    // NOTE: periodic re-apply is the WRONG stuck mechanism (see project
    // notes). The correct one is a write-path mask hook in
    // PhysRegFile::setReg. This periodic re-apply is kept only as a
    // fallback for the 'arch_*' modes' compatibility and will NOT make
    // stuck faults truly permanent on O3 for the same reason CHAOSReg's
    // didn't propagate (commits vs in-flight). Phys-fi_mode stuck also needs
    // the write-path mask to be correct.
```

Note this version sets `entry.second.update = false` after re-applying (CHAOSPhysReg.cc:363), i.e. it re-applies **only once** — so for CHAOSPhysReg the stuck fault is NOT truly permanent in the current code (the TODO is unfixed here, unlike CHAOSReg which was fixed).

### Read-trace hook (closure verification)

CHAOSPhysReg also arms a `readTraceEvent` after each inject (CHAOSPhysReg.cc:273-281) that polls `PhysRegFile::getReadsBeforeOverwrite()` every 100k cycles until all threads halt, to measure whether the injected cell is actually read before being overwritten.

