## Plain-English summary: how each one resolves the register to inject into

**CHAOSReg (original):** Picks a random **architectural register index** (uniform over `numRegs()` of the chosen class, optionally capped by `maxRegIdx`), then calls `ThreadContext::setReg(archRegId, corruptedValue)`. It never explicitly touches the rename map or the physical register file. On an in-order CPU this writes the live register correctly. On an O3 CPU, `ThreadContext::setReg` routes through the **commit-stage rename map** — so it writes the physical register that holds the *committed* value of that arch reg. In-flight instructions that were renamed earlier and will read this arch reg later have already been pointed at a *different* (older) physical register, so they read the **uncorrupted** old value. The fault therefore does not propagate to in-flight instructions on O3 — this is the "commit-map arch" abstraction and the reason it is considered incorrect for O3. Stuck-at-0/1 is a write-path mask re-applied by a periodic timer event (every `cyclesPermamedFaultCheck` cycles, default 1), not a hook on `setReg` — so a program write between two checks briefly overwrites the stuck value. (The original upstream code re-applied only once and then stopped, which made "permanent" faults not actually permanent; this repo fixes that by keeping `update = true`.)

**CHAOSPhysReg (new), three modes:**
- **`phys`** (default): picks a **physical register index** — either `targetPhysRegIdx` or random over `[0, numIntPhysRegs-1]` — and read-modify-writes that physical cell directly in `PhysRegFile`. It does not care which arch reg (if any) currently maps to the slot. Whoever is allocated to that phys cell next (or is holding it in-flight) reads the corrupted value. This is the ITC'23/GeFIN abstraction. It also probes the free list to report whether the slot was active or dead at inject time (liveness diagnostic).
- **`arch_frontend`**: picks an **architectural register index** (`targetArchRegIdx`), flattens it, looks it up in the **front-end rename map** (`renameMap.lookup`) to get the phys reg that in-flight instructions will read, then writes that phys reg directly. This is the "corrected arch" abstraction for O3 — it targets the phys reg actually mapped to the arch reg at the rename stage, so in-flight readers see the corruption.
- **`arch_commit`**: same as `arch_frontend` but looks up the **commit rename map** (`commitRenameMap.lookup`) instead — reproduces CHAOSReg's commit-map resolution, kept for comparison (explicitly noted as "fails on O3" in the .py). Note: even here the write goes to the phys file directly via `PhysRegFile::setReg`, not through `ThreadContext`, so it is not byte-for-byte identical to CHAOSReg.

Stuck-at in CHAOSPhysReg uses the same periodic-re-apply pattern keyed by phys idx, but the code explicitly says this is the **wrong** mechanism (a write-path mask hook on `PhysRegFile::setReg` is the correct one) and is a TODO. In CHAOSPhysReg `update` is set to `false` after the first re-apply (CHAOSPhysReg.cc:363), so its stuck faults are NOT truly permanent in the current code.

**O3 hooks (instrumented functions):**
- `cpu/o3/cpu.hh:465-475` — four accessors (`physRegFile`, `frontRenameMap`, `commitRenameMapAccess`, `physFreeList`) added to `o3::CPU` so CHAOSPhysReg can reach protected members. This is the only structural modification to the O3 CPU class itself.
- `cpu/o3/regfile.hh:209-225` (inside `PhysRegFile::getReg`, IntRegClass branch) — read counter increment for the read-trace/closure-verification feature.
- `cpu/o3/regfile.hh:312-324` (inside `PhysRegFile::setReg`, IntRegClass branch) — write-detection that stops the read counter.
- `cpu/o3/free_list.hh:109-126` (`SimpleFreeList::contains`) and `free_list.hh:212-218` (`UnifiedFreeList::isFree`) — liveness probe used by `phys` mode.

There is **no** instrumentation in `commit.cc`, `rename.cc`, `decode.cc`, `iew.cc`, or `scoreboard.cc`. Injection is entirely event-driven (a gem5 `Event` on the CPU's event queue) and reaches the pipeline state through the four accessors at inject time.

